WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.958
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.958 --> 00:00:27.610
 

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.210
DAVENPORT: At MIT,
there was always

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:32.420
a desire to elevate the arts.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.810
Jerry Wiesner very
specifically would say,

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:39.630
you can't do good
engineering without the arts.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.170
He really believed
that the arts were

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:46.030
integral to a good education.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.590
The Film Section actually
started because of a class

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:54.560
that Ed Pincus ran out of
his studio in Central Square.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.960
And so many students
at MIT signed up for it

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.290
that the powers that be decided
that they should move it

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.680
to the main campus, and find
a well known filmmaker who

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.030
could head the film section.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.825
And that was Ricky Leacock.

00:01:09.825 --> 00:01:11.840
LEACOCK: I was first
asked by Jerry Wiesner

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.040
to come teach Film/Video at MIT.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.870
I was very hesitant.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:20.800
He rephrased it, and
said, what do you

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.900
think that a place like
MIT could contribute

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.830
to the art of filmmaking?

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:31.000
And it's for this reason
that we take very seriously

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:37.751
the effect of technology
on the creation of movies.

00:01:37.751 --> 00:01:40.380
DAVENPORT: Ricky
is often described

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.670
as being the father
of cinema verite.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:47.440
He became a documentary
filmmaker very early in life.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.030
I think he made his
first film at age 14.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:52.750
LEACOCK: What I
love about the kind

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.820
of filming that I've
spent my life doing

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:58.340
is that there are
always surprises.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.140
You always have a
preconceived idea

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.350
of what you think
is going to happen.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.850
And it's as you see that
preconception disintegrating,

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:11.070
and the film becoming
something entirely different,

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.700
that you delight.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.200
DAVENPORT: The Film
Section actually

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.620
started as an
undergraduate program.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:20.620
It didn't take graduates until
three years into the program.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:24.250
So we had Introduction to
Filmmaking, Intermediate

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.180
Filmmaking, and
Advanced Filmmaking,

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.210
and students flocked to it.

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:32.420
So in terms of its relationship
with the student body,

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.835
it was a very
happening endeavor.

00:02:35.835 --> 00:02:38.670
 

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.510
Students loved the technology.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:43.070
They loved to be able
to express themselves.

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:45.740
And they loved the adventure,
I think, of going out of MIT

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:51.060
to try to tell a story that
wasn't in their immediate life.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.030
The students shared.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:54.650
They shared their techniques.

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:56.420
There was a very
relaxed feeling.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.090
But there was also
a driven feeling

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:02.550
of everyone wanting to
go out and make a film.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.860
LEACOCK: This film, it
was a wide open program

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.870
of asking people to let us film
aspects of their private lives

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.630
that they wanted filmed.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:14.130
This requires an
awful lot of energy

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:15.960
all the way through the term.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.180
DAVENPORT: Ricky wanted to
make double system equipment,

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:23.100
so that anyone could make a
documentary film that they

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.400
were interested in making.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.110
I came to MIT to
work on Super 8,

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.950
and I couldn't understand
why you couldn't

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:35.570
sync a sound recording that
you made on a tape recorder

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:40.110
with the film that came out
of a Super 8 film camera.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:41.130
MIT had the answer.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:51.250
 

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:54.390
The Film/Video section
became the venue for artists

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:58.850
to come to, and want to screen
their films for our audience.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:02.860
The Monday night screenings
were always open to the public.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:07.170
Mary Jane Doherty, who
made the funniest films

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:08.710
that were released.

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:13.114
I mean, she really had a
wonderful sense of humor.

00:04:13.114 --> 00:04:16.539
DOHERTY: This is an experimental
astrophysics laboratory.

00:04:16.539 --> 00:04:19.740
WEISSKOPF: Heavy double stars
that rotate around each other,

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:20.682
you know, and--

00:04:20.682 --> 00:04:23.223
DOHERTY: (WHISPERING) This is
a famous theoretical physicist,

00:04:23.223 --> 00:04:25.047
Dr. Victor Weisskopf.

00:04:25.047 --> 00:04:27.330
WEISSKOPF: --emit
gravity waves, and this

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.980
is what Ray is looking for.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:32.105
WEISS: So one of us has to
stand on the table, huh?

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:33.091
Or we stand on ladders.

00:04:33.091 --> 00:04:34.077
STUDENT: Well, two
ladders, actually.

00:04:34.077 --> 00:04:34.868
WEISS: Two ladders.

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:36.234
Wonderful.

00:04:36.234 --> 00:04:37.148
Heroics.

00:04:37.148 --> 00:04:39.513
You might as well go to
become Ringling Brothers.

00:04:39.513 --> 00:04:40.054
STUDENT: Yes.

00:04:40.054 --> 00:04:44.170
 

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:46.620
DAVENPORT: I think the
move to the Media Lab,

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.230
it was by choice.

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:53.200
But it was also
sort of self-evident

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.770
that we needed to go
there if we were going

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:59.060
to invent some of
the new technologies,

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:01.610
and the new approaches to video.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:04.400
And that's of course
what MIT is all about.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.850
It's about invention,
and moving on to the new.

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:12.190
In terms of Film/Video,
it was very clear

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:15.050
to me, and to a few
others, at the Section

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:18.240
at that time, that computers
were going to totally change

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:18.850
how we edited.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:38.250
 

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:43.550
Ricky himself, I think, was
not so interested in staying

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:45.170
long term at MIT.

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:48.590
He wanted to go off and make
more of the type of movies

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:54.180
he'd been talking about
using the Video 8 technology,

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.820
and just see what you
would make if you hung out

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:59.890
on the streets of Paris.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:02.660
One of the really
strong memories

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:05.688
I have of the Film Section,
and the Film/Video Section,

00:06:05.688 --> 00:06:09.300
and even my groups here, the
Interactive Cinema Group,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.360
are all the fantastic
people who went through.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.940
 

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:18.800
It really was remarkable,
particularly in the early days.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.610
I think there wasn't anything
like that in the country.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:25.610
There are many very accomplished
filmmakers who came out

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:31.190
of the Film Section-- Ross
McElwee, Rob Moss, Steve Asher,

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:36.080
Bernice Schneider, Alex
Anthony, Mary Jane Doherty,

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.010
Barry Strongin,
and just many more,

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:45.631
moving through into the days
of the Media Lab as well.

00:06:45.631 --> 00:06:48.080
Yeah, very influential.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:07:08.569
 

